{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1532743688,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDE3NTgzNzAyODk="},"added":["metadata"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1554705820,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ4MDcwNDM4MQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2715#issuecomment-480704381"},"message":"I think I am not able to decide in-code whether \"it is worth it\" given the fact that re-aggregation did not change any metadata across all metadata extractors, despite the datalad version string. Moreover, elsewhere there was the expressed demand to code extractor versions too. The proper place for this is the metadata itself (think `describedBy`). This would result in any re-aggregation with any version difference to result in a larger diff within the metadata itself (in unforeseeable places).\n\nhttps://github.com/datalad/datalad-revolution/pull/84 implements a slightly different approach. It uses a real `diff` call to check whether there where any metadata-relevant changes between the last aggregation state and the status quo. If there are none, no re-aggregation is performed, unless forced (in which case, one would always want a commit IMHO).\n\nIn general, with https://github.com/datalad/datalad-revolution/pull/84 metadata becomes much richer and the chances to observe the situation above are slim.","files":null}]}